1
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,600
He knew what she would do.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,200
He made the tree anyway.

3
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:21,600
And left it standing in the middle of the garden.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,400
The first answer is freedom.

5
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:30,200
A love that cannot refuse is not love.

6
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:33,800
Take the tree away and obedience becomes machinery.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,200
The second answer: knowing is not causing.

8
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,600
A lantern shows the path. It does not walk you down it.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,800
Foreknowledge sees. It does not push.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,200
The third answer: the command was the relationship.

11
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:54,200
One tree, one word — trust me about this one.

12
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,800
The serpent never argued about fruit. It argued about God's character.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,600
The fourth answer: removing it would have fixed nothing.

14
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,800
The temptation was never the tree.

15
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,800
Remove one occasion, and another grows in its place.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The fifth answer is stranger.

17
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:17,600
The cross was not an emergency.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,600
Some traditions call the fall fortunate: the rescue was planned before the garden.

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
These are traditions, not a proof.

20
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:35,200
Augustine, Aquinas, Boethius and the Eastern fathers each hold a piece of it.

21
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,000
The question stays open. Perhaps it was meant to.
